# DESCRIPTION

The time service sets the system's UTC.
On amd64 it reads the PC's real-time clock once, as it starts, and publishes that time in the UTC page the launcher gave it to write; every process maps the page, and `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)` reads it.
On the other architectures it finds no real-time clock to read, publishes nothing, and UTC stays at the launcher's starting value.

The clock is read through the CMOS ports 0x70 and 0x71.
The service waits out an update in progress, then reads the registers again until two readings agree (at most six).
It takes the registers as the clock's status register says they are kept, binary or BCD and a 24-hour or 12-hour clock, and the year from the century register, 0x32, where there is one and as 20YY where there is not.
The machine's clock is taken to be UTC.
The time is published with an error of one second, the clock's resolution.
A reading that is no date (a month 13, a 30th of February) is refused.

The manifest (manifest(6)) of a machine that has the clock:

```text
service time
program time
export
early
clock
resource ioport
```

`early` starts it before the services that read the time, `clock` gives it the UTC page to write and `resource ioport` the ports.
The service prints a line to the log when it has set the time, and exits with an error, saying why, if it has no page, no ports, a clock that says no date, or cannot write the page.

# EXAMPLES

On a machine whose clock reads 9:15:30 on 12 October 2026, reading `/svc/time/status` gives a line like this:

```rc
% cat /svc/time/status
set from rtc 2026-10-12T09:15:30Z utc 1791796530000000000
```

# FILES

: `/svc/time/status`
  What the service did: on amd64, `set from rtc `, the date it read as `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`, ` utc ` and the same time as nanoseconds since 1970; elsewhere `no rtc`.
  A line, ended by a newline.

